From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 18:10:00 -0700
Subject: mnt-sc: fix inverted "err " response check

sc_cmdresp_retry()'s response parser branches on whether the firmware's
reply starts with "err ":

	} else if (vcount >= 4 && strncmp(rxbuf, "err ", 4)) {
		dev_err(&mntsc->spi->dev, "mntsc: error response: %s\n", rxbuf);
	...
	} else {
		dev_err(&mntsc->spi->dev, "mntsc: couldn't parse response: %s\n", rxbuf);

strncmp() returns 0 when the strings match. Used directly as a boolean
here without negation, this condition is true when rxbuf does NOT start
with "err ", so a genuine firmware error response falls through to the
"couldn't parse response" branch instead of the intended "error
response" branch, mislabeling real firmware-reported errors as parse
failures (e.g. "mntsc: couldn't parse response: err maxp" and
"...err undf", observed on RK3588S/Radxa CM5 hardware).

Both branches otherwise do the same thing (log, set ret = -EAGAIN,
break), so this doesn't change retry behavior, only which message gets
logged - worth fixing anyway since it actively misleads whoever's
reading dmesg to debug a firmware-side error.

Signed-off-by: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
---
 drivers/firmware/mnt-sc.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/firmware/mnt-sc.c b/drivers/firmware/mnt-sc.c
index 8bcda2173b3b..2b3c4d5e6f70 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/mnt-sc.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/mnt-sc.c
@@ -413,7 +413,7 @@ static int sc_cmdresp_retry(struct mntsc_driver_data *mntsc, const char *cmd,
 							ret = -EAGAIN;
 							break;
 						}
-					} else if (vcount >= 4 && strncmp(rxbuf, "err ", 4)) {
+					} else if (vcount >= 4 && !strncmp(rxbuf, "err ", 4)) {
 						dev_err(&mntsc->spi->dev, "mntsc: error response: %s\n", rxbuf);
 						ret = -EAGAIN;
 						break;
--
